{
  "dom_id": "step-q28925",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $y={6}^{x}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The graph of $y={6}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept of $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x>0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x<0.$ \n\nWhen $x=1,$ we have $y={6}^{1}=6.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(1,6).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194531/assets/016f88de4ffd29f3b5721c767bf9e2db493ef7ac91dc2613ee7f3169dcba1718.png)",
